00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:57.690
- Okay, my name is Brian Grazer

00:01:57.690 --> 00:02:00.200
I accidented into the media business

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:03.030
into the storytelling
business, it wasn't a plan.

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:07.330
I didn't really know much about
movies or television at all.

00:02:07.330 --> 00:02:09.370
I grew up very very very middle-class

00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:12.140
in a little cul-de-sac in Sherman Oaks

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:13.807
and now all off a sudden--

00:02:13.807 --> 00:02:14.760
- And real quick, 'cause I love to jump in

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:17.480
and they are gonna be mad at
me for cutting you off already.

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:21.180
What did you play, as
a six to 13 year old?

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:22.013
Like what kind of kid were you?

00:02:22.013 --> 00:02:24.540
You were like sports, were you drawing?

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:25.790
Were you selling?

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:27.793
Were you lemonade stands, washing cards?

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:31.630
- I didn't do lemonade
stands, I would sell magazines

00:02:31.630 --> 00:02:34.720
all types, Life Magazine,
National Geographics

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:37.910
like lots of other kids, baseball cards.

00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:40.900
I did a, the Valley News And Green Sheet

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:43.950
which was a newspaper, voluntary payment.

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:46.810
I was literally delivering papers

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:48.920
to get no payment, zero.

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:50.460
So you have to knock on the door

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:52.527
and go, "Hi, my name is Brian Grazer

00:02:52.527 --> 00:02:54.387
"I deliver your Valley
News And Green Sheet

00:02:54.387 --> 00:02:55.220
"I know it's voluntary

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:58.140
"but would you consider
paying me for the month?"

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:01.320
And I'd get like 50% of them.

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:04.170
- How much of that selling
in that kind of environment

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:07.610
do you think later helped
you sell through ideas?

00:03:07.610 --> 00:03:09.850
- Tom Hanks, when he learned

00:03:09.850 --> 00:03:12.540
I delivered the Valley
News And Green Sheet

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:15.380
and because he is a master of all details.

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:16.937
He said, "This is the perfect thing

00:03:16.937 --> 00:03:18.097
"you're the perfect producer

00:03:18.097 --> 00:03:19.467
"that's what they have to do

00:03:19.467 --> 00:03:21.667
"sell sell sell sell sell

00:03:21.667 --> 00:03:25.637
"knowing they're always
gonna get baseline no.

00:03:25.637 --> 00:03:27.190
"How are you going to treat that?"

00:03:27.190 --> 00:03:30.207
And I used to say, "As
a producer, no to me

00:03:30.207 --> 00:03:31.587
"is just a temporary point of view.

00:03:31.587 --> 00:03:33.363
"I'll soon change that." (laughs)

00:03:33.363 --> 00:03:34.520
- I love that.

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:35.740
So you were that kinda kid?

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:37.520
So you played sports, you sold stuff?

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:39.700
- I played sports, I sold stuff.

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:42.743
I had multiple paper
outs (mumbles) just said.

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:46.863
I was a champion for the underdog always.

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:49.940
Probably 'cause I felt
like such an underdog

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:51.160
in the classroom itself.

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:54.340
So in the classroom, I had acute dyslexia

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:55.860
I couldn't read a word.

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:59.530
So I couldn't, certainly
I couldn't read a sentence

00:03:59.530 --> 00:04:03.266
none of the words lined
up, you know, cohesively

00:04:03.266 --> 00:04:06.410
or sequentially, I couldn't read at all

00:04:06.410 --> 00:04:09.200
and so therefore it
caused me a lot of shame

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:12.830
because I would have to try to avoid

00:04:12.830 --> 00:04:14.410
all this sort of shame
that would come with it

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:16.720
so I would look away when
the teacher looked at me.

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:19.200
So I'd never look at anyone
in the eyes in class.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:20.520
- I know that game very well.

00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:21.460
- Were you there?

00:04:21.460 --> 00:04:23.220
- You know, I never was diagnosed

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:25.900
but my ability to consume
information through reading

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:27.993
or my ability to read out loud.

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:31.210
If you ask me right now
to walk across the street

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:32.600
to go to Madison Square Garden

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:34.340
and talk in front of 35,000 people

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:35.173
on a dime?
(finger snaps)

00:04:35.173 --> 00:04:37.580
I would do it so easily.

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:40.530
If you asked me to read the
back of this Ken Griffey Jr.

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:42.347
I'd be like, literally
sitting here announcing

00:04:42.347 --> 00:04:44.510
"Don Drysdale pitched the
first no-hitter for the."

00:04:44.510 --> 00:04:46.865
Like, that is like not comfortable for me.

00:04:46.865 --> 00:04:47.790
- I understand. (laughing)

00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:50.170
- So I very much understand
that and back to your point

00:04:50.170 --> 00:04:51.440
never wanting to get called on

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:53.340
since I knew no answers to anything.

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:55.140
Nor did I ever wanna read in class

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:56.890
was something I mastered as well.

00:04:56.890 --> 00:04:59.520
- Yeah, so probably you did things like

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:01.840
invent having to go to the bathroom

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:04.483
when you knew it was coming
at you, you know, question.

00:05:04.483 --> 00:05:06.090
- I invented everything to avoid.

00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:07.559
- Yeah, okay, so we did it all.

00:05:07.559 --> 00:05:08.392
We did all the same stuff.

00:05:08.392 --> 00:05:09.330
- Not going to school at all

00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:10.720
like being homesick
(Brian laughs)

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:12.690
because I knew there was
an unavoidable situation

00:05:12.690 --> 00:05:14.160
like, the big test.

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:15.540
Yes, I did all those things.

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:17.250
- That seemed to work for you nonetheless.

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:18.100
- Yeah, but you know what?

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:20.240
It's an interesting starting
point to this conversation

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:23.340
because I think, right now we
live in a parenting culture

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:25.440
that tries to fix everything

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:28.240
and I think all the
advantages are from adversity.

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:29.880
- Yeah, me too, I totally agree.

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:31.950
- There's a lot of people
watching right now that, you know

00:05:31.950 --> 00:05:34.130
listen, everyone has their
strengths, weaknesses

00:05:34.130 --> 00:05:36.440
shortcomings, happiness's
but like professionally

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:38.460
you and I have had nice runs.

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:40.070
And I genuinely think a whole lot of that

00:05:40.070 --> 00:05:41.480
has to do with not being good at school.

00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:43.350
- For sure, I agree with you.
- Period.

00:05:43.350 --> 00:05:46.080
- Because it causes us to be self reliant

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:47.920
where in kids now and I have kids

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:52.510
they become disempowered.

00:05:52.510 --> 00:05:55.720
- 100% and you know what
happens with disempowerment?

00:05:55.720 --> 00:05:56.951
Unhappiness
- Unhappiness.

00:05:56.951 --> 00:05:57.784
- And what happens with unhappiness?

00:05:57.784 --> 00:05:59.090
- 'Cause you have no identity.
- Depression.

00:05:59.090 --> 00:06:01.450
- And so everybody wants
to blame social networks

00:06:01.450 --> 00:06:03.610
for all these bad things
that are happening to kids

00:06:03.610 --> 00:06:05.540
without taking a look at themselves

00:06:05.540 --> 00:06:07.400
