I mean I literally fought--

00:10:14.301 --> 00:10:15.940
- You're gonna get a lot of
comments on this episode.

00:10:15.940 --> 00:10:16.933
- I'm very aware.

00:10:17.980 --> 00:10:20.330
I literally was like,
literally had in sixth grade

00:10:20.330 --> 00:10:22.760
against Oded Weinstock,
that was the kid's name

00:10:22.760 --> 00:10:24.090
Oded Weinstock.

00:10:24.090 --> 00:10:24.923
- Wow, that's a name.

00:10:24.923 --> 00:10:28.410
- Like it was like a,
3:00 p.m. at Marco's house

00:10:28.410 --> 00:10:30.900
across from the ice cream truck

00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:32.280
and the whole school knew.

00:10:32.280 --> 00:10:35.510
Like the whole day, the
fight was getting promoted.

00:10:35.510 --> 00:10:37.233
Like I was Dan Kane or something.

00:10:37.233 --> 00:10:38.520
(laughing)

00:10:38.520 --> 00:10:39.840
- That's very funny, that's cool.

00:10:39.840 --> 00:10:40.895
- So you won this big fight?

00:10:40.895 --> 00:10:42.610
- So I won this big fight

00:10:42.610 --> 00:10:45.613
that became a part of, a
very big part of my identity.

00:10:50.243 --> 00:10:52.670
In Junior High I was able to read.

00:10:52.670 --> 00:10:55.680
Not well, kind of like your trepidation

00:10:55.680 --> 00:10:56.810
without reading that baseball card.

00:10:56.810 --> 00:10:58.450
Anyway, I was able to read.

00:10:58.450 --> 00:10:59.820
So at that moment in time

00:10:59.820 --> 00:11:01.770
I started really looking at people.

00:11:01.770 --> 00:11:04.110
I did just exactly the opposite

00:11:04.110 --> 00:11:05.270
and I started looking at people

00:11:05.270 --> 00:11:08.140
and realizing, wow, I
can learn so much more

00:11:08.140 --> 00:11:09.780
by actually looking at them.

00:11:09.780 --> 00:11:11.840
So not only am I reading some stuff

00:11:11.840 --> 00:11:15.580
which would be kind of a
ancillary sort of educational tool

00:11:15.580 --> 00:11:17.060
but the real educational tool

00:11:17.060 --> 00:11:20.360
is, like face-to-face communication.

00:11:20.360 --> 00:11:24.180
Really being present looking
at someone in the eyes

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:26.190
not staring at them in some weird way

00:11:26.190 --> 00:11:28.160
not trying to think of 20 questions

00:11:28.160 --> 00:11:29.690
but just like--
- Listening.

00:11:29.690 --> 00:11:30.670
- Listening, yeah.

00:11:30.670 --> 00:11:32.250
Like letting that evolve

00:11:32.250 --> 00:11:35.420
and I found that by
face-to-face, human connection

00:11:35.420 --> 00:11:38.820
I could ask questions
and really learn a lot.

00:11:38.820 --> 00:11:40.470
When you look at somebody

00:11:40.470 --> 00:11:42.540
they have a tendency to trust you

00:11:42.540 --> 00:11:45.270
they have a tendency to feel
validated as a human being

00:11:45.270 --> 00:11:48.640
they have a tendency to feel
like, this guy really cares.

00:11:48.640 --> 00:11:51.930
And because of that, people
will really let it go

00:11:51.930 --> 00:11:55.660
that they will give you
their heart and soul

00:11:55.660 --> 00:11:57.290
and you give them your heart and soul

00:11:57.290 --> 00:11:59.260
and a lot to be gained.

00:11:59.260 --> 00:12:01.720
So that was the point of
why I wrote this book.

00:12:01.720 --> 00:12:02.660
'cause I found that I can--

00:12:02.660 --> 00:12:03.850
- And lets tell everybody
the name of the book.

00:12:03.850 --> 00:12:05.127
- The name of the book is called

00:12:05.127 --> 00:12:07.930
"Face To Face: The Art
Of Human Connection."

00:12:07.930 --> 00:12:09.450
- And you thought this
at an incredible time

00:12:09.450 --> 00:12:11.010
because this is your superpower

00:12:11.010 --> 00:12:12.470
that you learned early

00:12:12.470 --> 00:12:16.500
and now it's a counter-cultural
move to a technology world.

00:12:16.500 --> 00:12:18.480
Like it's so in the zeitgeist right?

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:20.407
You knew that so many
people would here and say

00:12:20.407 --> 00:12:22.037
"Oh my God, I need that

00:12:22.037 --> 00:12:24.420
"or my kids need that or
my executives need that."

00:12:24.420 --> 00:12:26.783
'cause everyone's demonizing technology.

00:12:27.646 --> 00:12:29.210
Like I think it's perfect timing.

00:12:29.210 --> 00:12:30.800
- Yeah, I think they're so compatible.

00:12:30.800 --> 00:12:33.730
I mean, search is very
compatible with human connection

00:12:33.730 --> 00:12:35.550
because, I don't think you should

00:12:35.550 --> 00:12:37.100
know nothing about somebody.

00:12:37.100 --> 00:12:39.590
Unless you're just needing

00:12:39.590 --> 00:12:41.170
but I think, you know,
when you're going out

00:12:41.170 --> 00:12:42.890
to meet somebody, you should learn some.

00:12:42.890 --> 00:12:45.450
It's proper to learn something
about that human being.

00:12:45.450 --> 00:12:46.780
- You know what's funny about that Brian?

00:12:46.780 --> 00:12:49.750
I often say that technology for me

00:12:49.750 --> 00:12:52.330
is the gateway drug to the face-to-face.

00:12:52.330 --> 00:12:54.523
- [Brian] Oh very good,
I've felt similarly.

00:12:55.700 --> 00:12:58.330
- I think there's a lot of
people who are more old-school

00:12:58.330 --> 00:13:00.970
who are like you and I,
that love face-to-face

00:13:00.970 --> 00:13:03.260
that completely shit on technology

00:13:03.260 --> 00:13:04.960
and think it's an either or.

00:13:04.960 --> 00:13:08.567
I'm like, "No no no, I have
way more human interactions

00:13:08.567 --> 00:13:11.170
"than most people because I use it."

00:13:11.170 --> 00:13:12.210
But I'm a little bit different

00:13:12.210 --> 00:13:14.300
I actually don't research
anything on people

00:13:14.300 --> 00:13:15.900
I meet for the first time.

00:13:15.900 --> 00:13:19.690
I use social media to meet
people, we talk a little bit

00:13:19.690 --> 00:13:21.410
and then we'll have a meeting.

00:13:21.410 --> 00:13:23.940
I like coming in cold

00:13:23.940 --> 00:13:27.440
'cause I like listening and
watching, the same way you do

00:13:27.440 --> 00:13:29.600
and I feel that if I do reading.

00:13:29.600 --> 00:13:31.610
That I'm taking somebody
else's perspective

00:13:31.610 --> 00:13:34.770
and I'm already hedging my own observation

00:13:34.770 --> 00:13:36.700
based on somebody's two cents.

00:13:36.700 --> 00:13:39.180
It's amazing how little
I know about most people

00:13:39.180 --> 00:13:41.503
when I meet them, even very fancy people.

00:13:42.410 --> 00:13:44.010
Because I don't know a whole lot

00:13:45.029 --> 00:13:47.770
I like that restaurant that
we met in the first time

00:13:47.770 --> 00:13:48.980
of course I've heard your name

00:13:48.980 --> 00:13:50.600
but I wouldn't have been able to know

00:13:50.600 --> 00:13:52.750
as especially 'cause I wasn't Holly

00:13:52.750 --> 00:13:55.640
I didn't know a lot about the
whole Hollywood ecosystem.

00:13:55.640 --> 00:13:57.880
I liked what I felt.

00:13:57.880 --> 00:13:59.960
- Yeah, that's interesting.

00:13:59.960 --> 00:14:01.480
The funny thing, Okay, first of all

00:14:01.480 --> 00:14:03.000
I love everything you just said.

00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:03.833
I think it's a--
(laughing)

00:14:03.833 --> 00:14:04.813
No I do, it's amazing.

00:14:07.550 --> 00:14:09.080
People say how do you, you know

00:14:09.080 --> 00:14:11.160
how have you started such great talents

00:14:11.160 --> 00:14:13.780
or given them a break or
how do you pick writers?

00:14:13.780 --> 00:14:16.660
I mostly pick people
just out of their aura

00:14:16.660 --> 00:14:21.660
their energetic aura, that
to me is the number one

00:14:21.790 --> 00:14:24.580
sort of a litmus test for
me, do I feel their energy?

00:14:24.580 --> 00:14:26.388
Is that feeling right?

00:14:26.388 --> 00:14:28.700
And then I go deeper, but--

00:14:28.700 --> 00:14:29.900
- Let's make this fun for the audience

00:14:29.900 --> 00:14:31.240
from a content standpoint.

00:14:31.240 --> 00:14:34.770
Give me some hot takes,
on first time you met them

00:14:34.770 --> 00:14:36.390
the world didn't know who they were

00:14:36.390 --> 00:14:37.810
now a lot of people know.

00:14:37.810 --> 00:14:39.430
Give me a couple anecdotal

00:14:39.430 --> 00:14:44.120
like person and what you
felt in that first meeting?

00:14:44.120 --> 00:14:45.460
- Jeez, okay.

00:14:45.460 --> 00:14:48.210
Well, I'm gonna cut back to Isaac Asimov

00:14:48.210 --> 00:14:51.660
he's the most prolific science
fiction writer in the world.

00:14:51.660 --> 00:14:54.610
I was dying to meet Isaac
Asimov, I met he and his wife

00:14:54.610 --> 00:14:56.741
I flew across the country from
LA to New York to meet him

00:14:56.741 --> 00:14:58.020
I didn't have a lot of money

00:14:58.020 --> 00:15:00.820
and within the first five
minutes (clears throat)

00:15:00.820 --> 00:15:03.397
His wife said to him, "Isaac,
I don't think he knows

00:15:03.397 --> 00:15:06.100
"enough about us, I
think we should leave."

00:15:06.100 --> 00:15:08.530
So that wasn't a good situation

00:15:08.530 --> 00:15:11.490
but I learned, in my case I learned

00:15:11.490 --> 00:15:13.800
and so I learned, you know,
that I probably shouldn't.

00:15:13.800 --> 00:15:15.710
I think they might have detected a truth

00:15:15.710 --> 00:15:18.660
they might have detected,
he doesn't know enough

00:15:18.660 --> 00:15:20.780
and I'm probably radiated that.

00:15:20.780 --> 00:15:22.770
That was probably living within my aura.

00:15:22.770 --> 00:15:24.730
- What was the context of the meeting?

00:15:24.730 --> 00:15:27.740
- Well the context was,
there was a point in my life

00:15:27.740 --> 00:15:30.800
through 35 years, where I
still do this every two weeks

00:15:30.800 --> 00:15:33.470
reach out, do a cold call to meet somebody

00:15:33.470 --> 00:15:35.850
that's expert anything other
than what I do for a living.

00:15:35.850 --> 00:15:38.540
So it could be science,
medicine, politics, religion--

00:15:38.540 --> 00:15:41.900
- What did they think
when you reached out.

00:15:41.900 --> 00:15:45.280
Why did they say yes,
from your perspective?

00:15:45.280 --> 00:15:46.210
From your perspective.

00:15:46.210 --> 00:15:48.970
- My perspective is, most people

00:15:48.970 --> 00:15:50.640
when they're out of alignment, you know

00:15:50.640 --> 00:15:53.850
like their agendas are out
of alignment with yours.

00:15:53.850 --> 00:15:55.837
They might just revert back to the fact.

00:15:55.837 --> 00:15:58.470
"Well, the guy makes big movies."

00:15:58.470 --> 00:16:00.210
and therefore it's possible

00:16:00.210 --> 00:16:03.930
they can enlargen my message.

00:16:03.930 --> 00:16:06.017
That's always sort of latent in me.

00:16:06.017 --> 00:16:08.050
- You know where I'm going
with this series of questions?

00:16:08.050 --> 00:16:09.650
- No.
- The level of audacity

00:16:09.650 --> 00:16:14.220
to make that statement, from
her, is I'm fascinated by.

00:16:14.220 --> 00:16:15.570
- She did it though it was--

00:16:15.570 --> 00:16:17.749
- Yeah, no, I'm aware she did
it, you just told me the story

00:16:17.749 --> 00:16:21.860
I'm always fascinated by the level of--

00:16:21.860 --> 00:16:23.450
- It's very audacious--
- It's very audacious--

00:16:23.450 --> 00:16:25.230
- To say, "Were leaving."
- Correct.

00:16:25.230 --> 00:16:29.140
And for me it stems from
a seed of insecurity

00:16:29.140 --> 00:16:30.870
that leads to that audacious act

00:16:30.870 --> 00:16:32.580
and I was more curious of like

00:16:32.580 --> 00:16:34.660
what could have led to that?

00:16:34.660 --> 00:16:37.530
What did she think you were extracting

00:16:37.530 --> 00:16:38.650
that you know, like--

00:16:38.650 --> 00:16:39.930
- [Brian] I don't know.

00:16:39.930 --> 00:16:41.460
- I'm very fascinated by

00:16:41.460 --> 00:16:44.692
bad behavior, is really interesting to me

00:16:44.692 --> 00:16:49.520
because I am unbelievably
empathetic to hateful people

00:16:49.520 --> 00:16:52.870
because the level of pain that
is actually happening inside

00:16:52.870 --> 00:16:55.523
to get to bad behavior, is extreme.

00:16:56.590 --> 00:17:00.960
I think we hate on hate
and I'm empathetic to hate.

00:17:00.960 --> 00:17:02.270
- [Brian] Interesting.

00:17:02.270 --> 00:17:03.980
- Which is not a very popular
point of view right now

00:17:03.980 --> 00:17:06.080
everyone just wants a fight in our society

00:17:06.080 --> 00:17:08.870
and I actually think the
unlock to betterness.

00:17:08.870 --> 00:17:10.900
Is to understand why somebody can even get

00:17:10.900 --> 00:17:15.010
like why would somebody do that, you know?

00:17:15.010 --> 00:17:16.670
The people that I have come across

00:17:16.670 --> 00:17:18.600
and I came from a very
Eastern European family

00:17:18.600 --> 00:17:20.530
there's a lot of dynamics there.

00:17:20.530 --> 00:17:23.530
The weakest, seem the strongest.

00:17:23.530 --> 00:17:25.940
That seems like a power move by her?

00:17:25.940 --> 00:17:28.850
It's a bullshit move by her,
I think it's a weak move.

00:17:28.850 --> 00:17:30.730
- I'm sure you are right, yeah.

00:17:30.730 --> 00:17:32.080
Well you study a lot of things

00:17:32.080 --> 00:17:35.170
that I, some things we overlap
on and that's interesting

00:17:35.170 --> 00:17:36.003
I don't--

00:17:36.003 --> 00:17:37.770
- Anyway, so you learn
from that, you took that--

00:17:37.770 --> 00:17:38.821
- So that was bad

00:17:38.821 --> 00:17:39.654
(laughing)

00:17:39.654 --> 00:17:40.740
That was really bad.

00:17:40.740 --> 00:17:42.510
Okay, Norman Lear, amazing.

00:17:42.510 --> 00:17:46.710
He just exudes, he's 98 years
old, he exudes goodness.

00:17:46.710 --> 00:17:48.430
I met him 40 years ago

00:17:48.430 --> 00:17:50.270
when he was like the king of everything

00:17:50.270 --> 00:17:52.730
and I just had lunch
with him again last week.

00:17:52.730 --> 00:17:56.060
- What about somebody
that, a writer or an actor

00:17:56.060 --> 00:17:58.730
or a producer, or whatever
that, this was their break

00:17:58.730 --> 00:17:59.700
that you gave him the break

00:17:59.700 --> 00:18:00.930
and what do you see in that moment--

00:18:00.930 --> 00:18:01.800
- Bradley Cooper.

00:18:01.800 --> 00:18:02.780
- Good, that's good, let's go there.

00:18:02.780 --> 00:18:04.740
- Okay, so check this
out on Bradley Cooper.

00:18:04.740 --> 00:18:06.510
Because he's such a big deal now.

00:18:06.510 --> 00:18:09.410
I mean, he's a, you know
Oscar nominee, everything.

00:18:09.410 --> 00:18:12.026
Anyway, Bradley Cooper--
- Motherfucker can sing.

00:18:12.026 --> 00:18:13.470
I mean, he could rave it.

00:18:13.470 --> 00:18:14.510
- So Bradley Cooper--

00:18:14.510 --> 00:18:16.150
- He's uncomfortably handsome.

00:18:16.150 --> 00:18:17.800
Let's just get to the
punchline, so go ahead .

00:18:17.800 --> 00:18:20.410
- It was at least 15 years ago

00:18:21.582 --> 00:18:23.620
but he remembers this super well

00:18:23.620 --> 00:18:25.230
and he brought it up to
me only just recently.

00:18:25.230 --> 00:18:28.820
But basically, he was
late to a meeting with me

00:18:28.820 --> 00:18:31.130
and he thought, "Brian
Grazer, like I can't be late."

00:18:31.130 --> 00:18:34.410
He's out of breath, he's
starving, he's in my office.

00:18:34.410 --> 00:18:38.020
I could tell he's hungry
because I was eating in

00:18:38.020 --> 00:18:40.750
he was supposed to be, I'm
eating a tuna fish sandwich

00:18:40.750 --> 00:18:42.780
a giant one from Nate and Al's

00:18:42.780 --> 00:18:45.380
and I say, "Why don't
you eat half of this?"

00:18:45.380 --> 00:18:47.150
He goes, "No."

00:18:47.150 --> 00:18:48.930
I go ahead and insist, he
goes, "All right, thanks."

00:18:48.930 --> 00:18:51.620
He gobbled it up like bang.

00:18:51.620 --> 00:18:54.790
Now, smash cut, he's
Oscar, winning everything.

00:18:54.790 --> 00:18:57.316
- What did you feel in that meeting?

00:18:57.316 --> 00:18:58.910
- I just felt like it was the
humane thing to do, he was--

00:18:58.910 --> 00:19:01.310
- No no no, not from your
perspective, I apologize.

00:19:01.310 --> 00:19:02.207
Like what didn't you take from it?

00:19:02.207 --> 00:19:04.070
Like, "I think this kid's got something."

00:19:04.070 --> 00:19:07.420
Or he said something interesting
or who introduced you.

00:19:07.420 --> 00:19:10.100
What's interesting and you
were curious about that

00:19:10.100 --> 00:19:11.640
I would say that's an interesting one.

00:19:11.640 --> 00:19:15.260
- Somebody introduced me or made him

00:19:15.260 --> 00:19:18.010
heightened my awareness to Bradley Cooper.

00:19:18.010 --> 00:19:20.990
- Had he been in that random,
"Sex in the City" episode yet?

00:19:20.990 --> 00:19:22.270
- Oh my God.
- You, like that?

00:19:22.270 --> 00:19:23.157
Can you sub the Vee.

00:19:23.157 --> 00:19:26.104
- Tee give me something, I'm
sneakier than people think.

00:19:26.104 --> 00:19:26.937
- Geez, you are sneaky.

00:19:26.937 --> 00:19:28.390
- I am sneakier than people think.

00:19:28.390 --> 00:19:30.170
- How you can get that,
where does that come out?

00:19:30.170 --> 00:19:32.150
Where in your vocabulary?

00:19:32.150 --> 00:19:34.270
- I am a weird dude,
I pick up random shit.

00:19:34.270 --> 00:19:36.580
- So I don't know, I feel like am sorry

00:19:36.580 --> 00:19:37.850
you got me, stumped me on that one.

00:19:37.850 --> 00:19:39.060
- So that was the one that you recall?

00:19:39.060 --> 00:19:40.114
- Yeah, I just wanted to meet him.

00:19:40.114 --> 00:19:41.480
Because it was a good-looking--

00:19:41.480 --> 00:19:42.670
- How about you and Ron?

00:19:42.670 --> 00:19:44.400
- Ron Howard, amazing.

00:19:44.400 --> 00:19:45.233
- When did you guys meet?

00:19:45.233 --> 00:19:46.750
- The reason I partnered with him.

00:19:46.750 --> 00:19:49.620
There was no evidence he
could be a director, really.

00:19:49.620 --> 00:19:51.400
There was like no empirical evidence.

00:19:51.400 --> 00:19:53.970
I was just yelled out of
my window, I was a producer

00:19:53.970 --> 00:19:57.870
I was 26 years old, 27 years
old, on the Paramount lobby.

00:19:57.870 --> 00:20:01.330
I just produced some really
big movies for TV at 25.

00:20:01.330 --> 00:20:04.290
Like one was the 20-hour
miniseries on the 10 commandments.

00:20:04.290 --> 00:20:06.970
So I sort of got, like
people were, you know

00:20:06.970 --> 00:20:08.350
thought this guy's got something

00:20:08.350 --> 00:20:11.290
so now I've got something,
I've got a big office

00:20:11.290 --> 00:20:14.000
I yell out the window,
'cause I see Ron Howard

00:20:14.000 --> 00:20:14.850
he's the actor on "Happy Days".

00:20:14.850 --> 00:20:16.280
(laughing)

00:20:16.280 --> 00:20:18.497
I go, "Ron Howard, hey."

00:20:19.487 --> 00:20:22.840
I think it scared him,
'cause he kind of ran away

00:20:23.730 --> 00:20:25.027
and then I called his office and said

00:20:25.027 --> 00:20:27.337
"Hey, I just yelled out to Ron Howard

00:20:27.337 --> 00:20:29.810
"I know this is his office, can I meet?"

00:20:29.810 --> 00:20:31.390
I sort of organized a meeting.

00:20:31.390 --> 00:20:34.340
He came up to my office,
like three or four days later

00:20:34.340 --> 00:20:37.120
and he just came in, I
almost felt like a glow

00:20:37.120 --> 00:20:41.520
like the glow of the sun,
coming in at me close up.

00:20:41.520 --> 00:20:44.720
Like just a glow of goodness
and I thought, "Wow."

00:20:44.720 --> 00:20:47.730
It was such a unique
feeling, energetically

00:20:47.730 --> 00:20:50.150
to experience and to accept.

