00:24:02.258 --> 00:24:04.200
- Because I rationalize, well the point is

00:24:04.200 --> 00:24:09.200
is that, anytime I make a
decision, that's a good decision

00:24:09.580 --> 00:24:11.100
that means it's shitty.

00:24:11.100 --> 00:24:12.710
Every decision has to be excellent.

00:24:12.710 --> 00:24:14.950
If it's not excellent
and I go, "Good enough."

00:24:14.950 --> 00:24:16.410
It always turns into shitty.

00:24:16.410 --> 00:24:19.440
- Let me ask you a random
thing in the other one.

00:24:19.440 --> 00:24:21.720
This is a fun question,
I really want you to

00:24:21.720 --> 00:24:23.970
I hope there's a real answer to it.

00:24:23.970 --> 00:24:28.970
What film or show, honestly did way better

00:24:29.110 --> 00:24:30.910
than you expected it?

00:24:30.910 --> 00:24:32.240
'Cause we're all humans.

00:24:32.240 --> 00:24:33.527
Even in the 100 point scale, you're like

00:24:33.527 --> 00:24:35.200
"Oh, this is gonna be an 82."

00:24:35.200 --> 00:24:38.360
And then it was a 130, in reverse.

00:24:38.360 --> 00:24:41.030
'Cause it's easy to say
I fucked this up, right?

00:24:41.030 --> 00:24:44.037
Reverse, like deeply, like, you know

00:24:44.037 --> 00:24:45.797
you never had to publicly say like

00:24:45.797 --> 00:24:50.797
"Oh shit, this is that big, I
thought it was gonna be okay."

00:24:51.540 --> 00:24:52.373
- Oh that's so great.

00:24:52.373 --> 00:24:53.299
- That's a good question, right?

00:24:53.299 --> 00:24:54.132
- That's a really good question.

00:24:54.132 --> 00:24:56.970
No one's asked me that
question ever, no one.

00:24:56.970 --> 00:24:58.740
Very good, GaryVee.

00:24:58.740 --> 00:25:00.500
- You know why, 'cause it's
an interesting question.

00:25:00.500 --> 00:25:02.130
I know that you thought things

00:25:02.130 --> 00:25:05.010
that then did better, I
mean it's just the truth

00:25:05.010 --> 00:25:06.610
it's just the way it actually works.

00:25:06.610 --> 00:25:08.830
- So you wanna know,
which movie did I think

00:25:08.830 --> 00:25:10.294
was gonna not do well--

00:25:10.294 --> 00:25:12.703
- Or by the way, it might have been a hit.

00:25:13.621 --> 00:25:14.740
It might have been a smash hit

00:25:14.740 --> 00:25:15.930
you thought it was gonna be a hit

00:25:15.930 --> 00:25:17.088
it's your biggest one of all time.

00:25:17.088 --> 00:25:21.171
Or, you thought it was
gonna be C and it was a B+.

00:25:21.171 --> 00:25:22.460
- I have a few of those for sure

00:25:22.460 --> 00:25:23.540
but I'm gonna give you the--

00:25:23.540 --> 00:25:24.807
- The biggest one.
- The biggest one.

00:25:24.807 --> 00:25:26.300
"Apollo 13".

00:25:26.300 --> 00:25:30.380
Because what happened
is, we got really into it

00:25:30.380 --> 00:25:34.150
Ron Howard, Tom Hanks
and I and we all thought.

00:25:34.150 --> 00:25:36.900
Everyone knows the ending, how can this do

00:25:36.900 --> 00:25:39.030
more than $40 million, literally.

00:25:39.030 --> 00:25:40.650
- Was the number you were
running or in your head.

00:25:40.650 --> 00:25:41.640
- That was the number, we bet

00:25:41.640 --> 00:25:43.800
we all wrote on a little piece of paper.

00:25:43.800 --> 00:25:44.910
The highest number

00:25:44.910 --> 00:25:45.743
was $40 million.
- Of the three of you, right?

00:25:45.743 --> 00:25:47.580
- It was 40.
- Was that yours?

00:25:47.580 --> 00:25:49.210
'Cause you're the most
optimistic as a salesman?

00:25:49.210 --> 00:25:51.237
- Yeah
- I knew it, go ahead and?

00:25:52.100 --> 00:25:53.617
- Well did like almost 400

00:25:53.617 --> 00:25:55.350
and it got nine Oscar nominations.

00:25:55.350 --> 00:25:56.837
- We know what happened

00:25:56.837 --> 00:25:59.140
- But so we were just like totally shocked

00:25:59.140 --> 00:26:01.870
we didn't realize it
was gonna become like--

00:26:01.870 --> 00:26:04.110
- Why did that movie happen?

00:26:04.110 --> 00:26:04.943
Do you remember?

00:26:04.943 --> 00:26:05.776
- Yeah of course I do.

00:26:07.400 --> 00:26:10.530
It happened because there was
a little 12 page treatment

00:26:10.530 --> 00:26:13.220
that Jim Lovell, the
actual astronaut wrote.

00:26:13.220 --> 00:26:15.580
I got it and I thought

00:26:15.580 --> 00:26:18.523
wow, this is like a
great story of survival.

00:26:19.755 --> 00:26:22.360
Because I'm really fascinated
with human capability

00:26:22.360 --> 00:26:24.060
and I thought, here are these guys

00:26:24.060 --> 00:26:27.190
they're all on the level of
human capability, level 10's

00:26:27.190 --> 00:26:30.780
all three of them and this event occurs

00:26:30.780 --> 00:26:33.167
that challenges that level 10

00:26:33.167 --> 00:26:36.993
and causes them to think,
we are not gonna make it.

00:26:38.606 --> 00:26:41.010
How do we summon something inside of us

00:26:41.010 --> 00:26:44.200
to get us to survive, when we
don't think it really exists.

00:26:44.200 --> 00:26:46.860
So I just thought, in the
world of like human capability

00:26:46.860 --> 00:26:50.060
with highly capable men,
that's what happened.

00:26:50.060 --> 00:26:51.097
And I thought that was pretty amazing.

00:26:51.097 --> 00:26:51.980
- That's cool.

00:26:51.980 --> 00:26:54.240
- Yeah, so that's what happened.

00:26:54.240 --> 00:26:55.610
I mean, of course it did really well

00:26:55.610 --> 00:26:58.350
but I thought there's no chance

00:26:58.350 --> 00:27:00.140
this is gonna do more than $40 million.

00:27:00.140 --> 00:27:01.690
- When did it open?

00:27:01.690 --> 00:27:03.590
December?
- 1897.

00:27:03.590 --> 00:27:05.290
- Do you remember?
- Ow, in July.

00:27:06.180 --> 00:27:07.700
I didn't realize this, 'cause we don't

00:27:07.700 --> 00:27:09.660
plan movies for release times.

00:27:09.660 --> 00:27:13.600
It synced up with NASA's
celebration of the 20th anniversary

00:27:13.600 --> 00:27:14.470
I think.
- Which was nice.

00:27:14.470 --> 00:27:16.000
- Yeah, which was nice, so that, we had--

00:27:16.000 --> 00:27:17.490
- All right, so Veronica says this is shit

00:27:17.490 --> 00:27:18.850
and so what do you then do?

00:27:18.850 --> 00:27:20.880
- So she says it's shit and then I go

00:27:20.880 --> 00:27:23.250
I don't know what to do,
I can't revisit this.

00:27:23.250 --> 00:27:25.860
I can't say I hate this thing already.

00:27:25.860 --> 00:27:27.130
She goes, "I'll help you."

00:27:27.130 --> 00:27:30.360
And so for a year, she
plugged in she helped me

00:27:30.360 --> 00:27:33.850
and really did quite a
bit, to say the least.

00:27:33.850 --> 00:27:35.520
- You know what's crazy about that

00:27:35.520 --> 00:27:38.320
like having people plug
in, I've said this publicly

00:27:38.320 --> 00:27:40.300
but I've never really gone all the way.

00:27:40.300 --> 00:27:43.000
I always love talking about how I passed

00:27:43.000 --> 00:27:44.720
on Uber twice, in the Angel round

00:27:44.720 --> 00:27:47.600
and cost myself like, you
know, half a billion dollars.

00:27:47.600 --> 00:27:49.290
And if you read my first book, "Crush It"

00:27:49.290 --> 00:27:51.440
that kind of put me on a map.

00:27:51.440 --> 00:27:54.930
The only person I thank,
besides my family is Travis.

00:27:54.930 --> 00:27:57.310
Travis was in between
things, just hanging out

00:27:57.310 --> 00:27:59.000
flying around the country,
going to conferences

00:27:59.000 --> 00:28:01.680
and in Vegas with me and
AJ, just hanging out.

00:28:01.680 --> 00:28:03.090
And he was like, "Hey, let me read it."

00:28:03.090 --> 00:28:05.780
And truly out of all my books

00:28:05.780 --> 00:28:08.210
the only thing that
has ever been affected.

00:28:08.210 --> 00:28:09.290
It's not Stephanie land

00:28:09.290 --> 00:28:12.700
though without her
constructing there is no books.

00:28:12.700 --> 00:28:14.900
But Travis gave me a lot of feedback

00:28:14.900 --> 00:28:16.600
of like, "No, but you say, you say this."

00:28:16.600 --> 00:28:18.680
And so I understand that dynamic.

00:28:18.680 --> 00:28:20.330
- Yeah, oh good yeah.

00:28:20.330 --> 00:28:22.380
So that's kinda how it
played out, so it was great.

00:28:22.380 --> 00:28:25.280
It was great, like my wife
helped me, it was awesome.

00:28:26.250 --> 00:28:28.670
- What are you hoping
people get out of it?

00:28:28.670 --> 00:28:31.310
- Well, I'm hoping that
people get out of it

00:28:31.310 --> 00:28:33.480
that anyone could do this.

00:28:33.480 --> 00:28:37.550
I was a total solid F student, solid

00:28:37.550 --> 00:28:40.310
- Do you think deeply
introverted people can do it?

00:28:40.310 --> 00:28:41.190
- Anyone can do this.

00:28:41.190 --> 00:28:42.023
- Okay keep going.

00:28:42.023 --> 00:28:45.160
- Anyone can do this,
because you don't have to be

00:28:45.160 --> 00:28:45.993
in other words--

00:28:45.993 --> 00:28:46.826
- What's stopping people
from not being good at--

00:28:46.826 --> 00:28:49.820
- This is how people screw
up on public speaking

00:28:52.360 --> 00:28:53.720
People are terrified of public speaking

00:28:53.720 --> 00:28:56.980
if you think to yourself, this is a gift

00:28:58.570 --> 00:29:00.770
thank the audience, be generous--

00:29:00.770 --> 00:29:01.970
- Why do you think people are scared

00:29:01.970 --> 00:29:03.570
of public speaking, so much?

00:29:03.570 --> 00:29:04.950
As like one of the core.

00:29:04.950 --> 00:29:08.030
- Shame, shame in front
of a lot of people.

00:29:08.030 --> 00:29:10.470
Their fear of being embarrassed.

00:29:10.470 --> 00:29:12.700
- Do you think that that
gets to the higher level

00:29:12.700 --> 00:29:14.790
of the concept of actually

00:29:14.790 --> 00:29:16.980
worrying about other people's judgment?

00:29:16.980 --> 00:29:18.730
- Yes. (laughing)

00:29:18.730 --> 00:29:20.600
- This is the shit I'm
spending all my time on.

00:29:20.600 --> 00:29:22.694
- That's a really good, that
is the most important thing.

00:29:22.694 --> 00:29:23.600
- Lemme give you an example.

00:29:23.600 --> 00:29:25.510
You and I as creative people.

00:29:25.510 --> 00:29:28.270
Do you understand that
kids are growing up now

00:29:28.270 --> 00:29:30.360
with TikTok, YouTube, Spotify.

00:29:30.360 --> 00:29:34.150
Like, do you understand that
everybody can create now?

00:29:34.150 --> 00:29:35.470
At scale?
- Yeah I do, I do.

00:29:35.470 --> 00:29:38.230
- At a level that is so unprecedented.

00:29:38.230 --> 00:29:39.950
There were so many gatekeepers

00:29:39.950 --> 00:29:41.720
when you got in the Hollywood game

00:29:41.720 --> 00:29:43.850
that had to say, "Yes you have a chance."

00:29:43.850 --> 00:29:46.810
Now we all have a chance
and yet people are still

00:29:46.810 --> 00:29:49.490
not making content to live their happiest

00:29:49.490 --> 00:29:54.040
and dream-filled life, out
of an anonymous comment

00:29:54.040 --> 00:29:55.802
that says they're ugly.

00:29:55.802 --> 00:29:56.635
- Yeah.

00:29:57.690 --> 00:29:58.523
That's crazy.

00:29:58.523 --> 00:30:00.400
- So I've been thinking a
lot about public speaking

00:30:00.400 --> 00:30:04.851
and social media, because I'm
obviously remarkable at both.

00:30:04.851 --> 00:30:06.621
(laughing)

00:30:06.621 --> 00:30:10.260
And what I figured out was
something quite ridiculous.

00:30:10.260 --> 00:30:13.630
My over-indexing, without jokes now

00:30:13.630 --> 00:30:15.170
in both those categories

00:30:15.170 --> 00:30:17.710
actually leads to the core super power

00:30:17.710 --> 00:30:20.220
that the DNA in parenting I was given was

00:30:20.220 --> 00:30:23.960
which was, I have such
internal self-esteem

00:30:23.960 --> 00:30:25.270
noise from the outside

00:30:25.270 --> 00:30:28.400
by the way, it's also why I have humility.

00:30:28.400 --> 00:30:30.760
I don't hear the cheering

00:30:30.760 --> 00:30:32.830
which allows me to not hear the booing.

00:30:32.830 --> 00:30:36.040
I believe that parents
are over cheering kids.

00:30:36.040 --> 00:30:38.300
People then become
addicted to the cheering--

00:30:38.300 --> 00:30:39.280
- That's a great subject.

00:30:39.280 --> 00:30:41.890
- Which then leads them to
be crippled by the booing.

00:30:41.890 --> 00:30:44.370
- Yeah, I agree.

00:30:44.370 --> 00:30:47.333
What you just said was
profound, I love it.

00:30:49.700 --> 00:30:53.060
That's why I say, the great
ones in the media, our business

00:30:53.060 --> 00:30:54.827
they go, "I don't read the good ones

00:30:54.827 --> 00:30:57.127
"I don't read the bad,
I don't read anything."

00:30:59.647 --> 00:31:01.610
The great human beings to me

00:31:01.610 --> 00:31:03.330
you know, the really self actualized ones

